myelofibrosis in association with pulmonary arterial hypertension</article-title><source>Ann Intern Med</source><year>2005</year><volume>143</volume><fpage>466</fpage><lpage>467</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16172450</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B18"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Popat</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Frost</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Guan</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Durette</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Reddy</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Prchal</surname><given-names>JT</given-names></name><article-title>High levels of circulating CD34 cells, dacrocytes, clonal hematopoiesis, and JAK2 mutation differentiate myelofibrosis with myeloid metaplasia from secondary myelofibrosis associated with pulmonary hypertension</article-title><source>Blood</source><year>2006</year><volume>107</volume><fpage>3486</fpage><lpage>3488</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1182/blood-2005-08-3319</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16418333</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>